  "content": {
    "id": "p34-q23-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "23",
    "prompt": "",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p34-q23-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Write down the first nine positive integers in base 3.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$1_3,\\ 2_3,\\ 10_3,\\ 11_3,\\ 12_3,\\ 20_3,\\ 21_3,\\ 22_3,\\ 100_3$.",
          "worked": "Count using the digits $0$, $1$ and $2$, carrying to the next place after $2$:\n$$1_3,\\ 2_3,\\ 10_3,\\ 11_3,\\ 12_3,\\ 20_3,\\ 21_3,\\ 22_3,\\ 100_3.$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 20
      },
      {
        "id": "p34-q23-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Square each of these numbers, writing your answer in base 3.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "In order: $1_3,\\ 11_3,\\ 100_3,\\ 121_3,\\ 221_3,\\ 1100_3,\\ 1211_3,\\ 2101_3,\\ 10000_3$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}(1_3)^2 &= 1_3\\\\(2_3)^2 &= 4_{10}=11_3\\\\(10_3)^2 &= 9_{10}=100_3\\\\(11_3)^2 &= 16_{10}=121_3\\\\(12_3)^2 &= 25_{10}=221_3\\\\(20_3)^2 &= 36_{10}=1100_3\\\\(21_3)^2 &= 49_{10}=1211_3\\\\(22_3)^2 &= 64_{10}=2101_3\\\\(100_3)^2 &= 81_{10}=10000_3.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 76
      },
      {
        "id": "p34-q23-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "What do you notice about the last non-zero digit of each of these answers? Will this always happen?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": {
            "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
            "version": 1,
            "blocks": [
              {
                "id": "p34-q23-c-short-result",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "The last non-zero digit is always 1. Yes, this holds for every positive integer square in base 3.",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "id": "p34-q23-c-short-method",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Remove trailing zeroes before squaring. The remaining integer ends in 1 or 2, whose squares both end in 1.",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          "worked": "The last non-zero digit is $1$ in every answer.\n\nWrite any positive integer as $n=3^k m$, where $m$ is not divisible by $3$. Its base-3 representation ends in $1$ or $2$. Since $1^2=1_3$ and $2^2=11_3$, the square $m^2$ ends in $1$.\n$$n^2=3^{2k}m^2.$$\nMultiplication by $3^{2k}$ appends $2k$ zeroes in base 3. Therefore, the last non-zero digit of $n^2$ is always $1$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 60
      },
      {
        "id": "p34-q23-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "Use this observation to prove that $\\sqrt{2}$ is not a rational number.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": {
            "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
            "version": 1,
            "blocks": [
              {
                "id": "p34-q23-d-short-result",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "\\sqrt{2}",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": " is irrational.",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "id": "p34-q23-d-short-method",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "If ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "\\sqrt{2}=\\frac{p}{q}",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": ", then ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "p^2=2q^2",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": ". In base 3 the two sides have last non-zero digits 1 and 2 respectively, a contradiction.",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          "worked": "Suppose $\\sqrt{2}$ is rational. Then $\\sqrt{2}=\\frac{p}{q}$ for positive integers $p$ and $q$.\n$$\\begin{align*}p^2 &= (\\sqrt{2}\\,q)^2\\\\p^2 &= 2q^2.\\end{align*}$$\nBy part (c), the last non-zero digit of $p^2$ in base 3 is $1$. The last non-zero digit of $q^2$ is also $1$, so doubling $q^2$ makes its last non-zero digit $2$: doubling that digit produces no carry, and trailing zeroes remain zeroes.\n\nThus $p^2$ and $2q^2$ cannot be equal. This contradiction proves that $\\sqrt{2}$ is irrational.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 68
      }
    ]
  },
